h('Executive Summary')
p('SEA IT SOLVED is a web-based classroom mathematics learning workspace that preserves evidence from a live lesson and turns it into instructor-approved learning resources. The revised system no longer depends on a single capture-to-cloud workflow. It supports controlled whiteboard capture, image-based mathematical recognition, lesson audio recording and transcription, context review, AI-assisted lesson preparation, quiz generation and editing, student delivery, solution activities, and class analytics. The instructor remains the decision-maker at every publication point.')
p('The project is intended for mathematics instructors, students, and administrators in the School of Engineering and Architecture of Holy Angel University. Its central contribution is a traceable instructional workflow: captured classroom evidence is reviewed before it becomes lesson material; generated content is editable and not published automatically; and students receive only instructor-approved materials and assessments. This structure addresses impermanent whiteboard content without treating artificial intelligence output as authoritative.')
p('The prototype uses a React and Vite frontend, a Node.js and Express backend, PostgreSQL data storage, role-based authentication, Gemini-backed recognition and reasoning services, browser-compatible camera and microphone adapters, controlled file storage, and export services. Evaluation will measure functional suitability, reviewed-content quality, response time, reliability, usability, student access, privacy, and responsible AI safeguards.')
h('1 Revised Thesis Proposal')
h('1.1 Background and Rationale',2)
p('Mathematics lessons frequently develop through worked examples, symbols, annotations, and corrections written in real time. A physical whiteboard enables an instructor to adapt explanations to the class, but its content can be erased before students have completed notes or before absent students can access the lesson. This challenge is particularly important in engineering mathematics, where a missing intermediate step can make a derivation difficult to reconstruct independently.')
p('The original SEA IT SOLVED proposal concentrated on a camera, optical character recognition, a chatbot dashboard, PDF generation, and a Google Drive upload. The implemented system has matured into a more complete academic workspace. It connects lesson capture and recognition with instructor review, structured lesson material, classroom quizzes, student practice, submissions, and analytics. This revision therefore redefines the project as an instructor-governed learning workflow rather than an automatic note generator.')
p('Research on handwritten mathematical expression recognition confirms that recognition quality can be affected by handwriting variation, spatial structure, lighting, glare, and symbol ambiguity. These limitations make human review essential. The revised design treats AI recognition as an assistive input and preserves the original capture and review context so an instructor can correct or reject uncertain output before learners receive it.')
h('1.2 Statement of the Problem',2)
p('Engineering mathematics classes need a practical way to preserve classroom evidence and prepare usable learning resources without asking instructors to abandon the physical whiteboard. General-purpose capture tools preserve images but often do not provide a coherent workflow for mathematical interpretation, content review, controlled publication, student assessment, and follow-up learning. Fully automatic AI-generated notes also create risks of mathematical error, incomplete context, and unverified publication.')
p('This study asks how a role-based web system can help instructors capture and review mathematics lesson evidence, develop accurate and readable learning materials, create aligned assessments, and monitor student learning while retaining human authority over all material released to students.')
h('1.3 Research Questions',2)
for z in ['How can the system capture and preserve whiteboard images and lesson audio in a form suitable for later review?','How can image recognition, transcription, and instructor context review be combined to prepare reliable mathematics lesson content?','How can instructors edit, approve, export, and publish lesson material without automatically exposing unreviewed AI output to students?','How can the system support quiz authoring, student attempts, solution activities, and feedback that remain connected to the lesson context?','How usable, reliable, secure, and responsive is the system for instructors, students, and administrators in the intended environment?','How well do the safeguards support privacy, accountability, and traceability when AI services process classroom materials?']:n(z)
h('1.4 Purpose and Objectives',2)
p('The purpose of this study is to design, develop, and evaluate SEA IT SOLVED as an AI-assisted classroom mathematics capture and learning workspace for the School of Engineering and Architecture. The system will help instructors transform reviewed classroom evidence into approved learning resources and assessments while enabling students to access their classes, materials, quizzes, and feedback through role-appropriate interfaces.')
for z in ['Develop controlled capture, calibration, lighting, image preprocessing, audio recording, and transcription capabilities suitable for classroom use.','Implement a review workflow in which instructors inspect recognized mathematics, transcripts, lesson context, and generated materials before publication.','Provide AI-assisted lesson preparation, document editing, structured exports, and focused lesson chat that operate on selected lesson context.','Provide instructor-managed sections, student enrollment through class codes, quiz authoring, randomized attempts, solution activities, review, and analytics.','Implement role-based authentication, instructor approval, email verification, bounded uploads, protected media access, and rate-limited AI requests.','Evaluate the prototype against applicable software quality, AI governance, information security, privacy, and data quality requirements.']:b(z)
h('1.5 Significance of the Study',2)
p('For instructors, SEA IT SOLVED reduces the administrative gap between a live lesson and a reusable learning resource. The platform preserves lesson evidence, provides a single review workspace, and supports structured material and quiz preparation. For students, it provides access to instructor-approved materials, quizzes, attempt history, solution activities, and feedback within the class they joined. For administrators, instructor approval and user management create a clear control point over institutional access.')
p('For the institution, the project offers a prototype that can be evaluated without replacing classroom teaching practice with an opaque automatic system. The workflow can support students who miss a session or need to revisit multi-step derivations, while keeping the instructor responsible for mathematical correctness and publication decisions.')
h('1.6 Scope and Delimitations',2)
p('The study covers the SEA IT SOLVED prototype implemented as a web platform. It includes instructor, student, and administrator roles; registration and authentication; instructor approval; class sections and join codes; lesson capture; whiteboard-image recognition; lesson audio recording and transcription; lesson context review; AI-assisted material preparation and quiz generation; document and PDF-oriented exports; student quizzes and solution activities; feedback; and analytics. The system is designed for mathematics-focused instruction, especially engineering mathematics where symbolic content and worked solutions are common.')
p('The prototype does not claim to replace an instructor, guarantee recognition of every mathematical diagram or handwriting style, or provide autonomous grading or publication. Recognition and generative outputs may contain errors and require instructor review. Performance depends on camera alignment, capture quality, internet availability, model-service availability, and classroom conditions. The research evaluation is limited to selected pilot participants and use scenarios.')
h('1.7 Conceptual Framework',2)
p('The revised framework follows an evidence-review-publish-learn cycle. Classroom evidence is captured through image and audio inputs. The system prepares recognition and transcription outputs, which the instructor reviews and may edit. The instructor then prepares and approves lesson material and assessments. Students consume approved resources, complete activities, and generate learning evidence that instructors can review through assessment and analytics interfaces.')
table(['Stage','Primary Actor','System Output','Control'],[['Capture','Instructor','Whiteboard images and optional audio','Calibration, lighting, capture preflight, upload limits'],['Understand','Instructor','Recognition, transcript, and lesson context','Uncertainty indicators and editable review'],['Prepare','Instructor','Lesson document, exports, and quiz draft','Context-bound AI assistance and targeted editing'],['Publish','Instructor','Approved materials and quizzes for a section','Explicit release decision and role authorization'],['Learn','Student','Materials, quiz attempts, solution activities, feedback','Enrollment and protected-route access'],['Evaluate','Instructor/Admin','Attempt history, reviews, and analytics','Role-specific access and audit-ready records']])
h('1.8 Revised System Workflow',2)
p('A lesson begins when an approved instructor opens a section and prepares the capture environment. Camera calibration and optional lighting controls help establish a usable visual input. The instructor may capture whiteboard evidence and record lesson audio. The backend stores and processes submissions through controlled capture, recognition, transcription, and job workflows.')
p('The instructor opens lesson context review to compare captures, recognized content, and transcript information. The instructor may correct context, reopen incomplete work, or keep only evidence that should guide material generation. AI services are used after a bounded context has been assembled; the resulting material remains editable and is not automatically presented as final instruction.')
p('After reviewing generated lesson material, the instructor can export an approved document, create an aligned quiz, set question and problem-solving options, edit generated questions, and release activities to the appropriate section. Students access only resources associated with enrolled sections. Their attempts and solution activities are recorded for instructor review and section-level analytics.')
h('2 Software Quality and AI Design Elements')
h('2.1 System Architecture',2)
p('SEA IT SOLVED uses a client-server architecture. The React frontend provides distinct interfaces for public access, instructors, students, and administrators. A Node.js and Express backend exposes authenticated APIs and coordinates database access, media storage, background workers, recognition providers, transcription providers, lesson generation, quiz generation, and export services. PostgreSQL stores user, section, lesson, quiz, and activity records. Protected media and short-lived processing files are handled through storage abstractions and guarded download paths.')
table(['Layer','Implemented Responsibility','Examples'],[['Presentation','Responsive web screens and role-specific workflows','React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, protected routes'],['Application','Authentication, authorization, lesson, quiz, section, and admin APIs','Express controllers, services, middleware'],['AI and processing','Recognition, transcription, lesson intelligence, quiz generation, context-aware chat','Gemini providers, background workers, request gates'],['Data and files','Transactional records, temporary processing files, protected media, export records','PostgreSQL, storage provider abstraction'],['Hardware integration','Browser camera and microphone controls with mock and Raspberry Pi adapters','Capture, lighting, camera, microphone services']])
h('2.2 User Roles and Access Boundaries',2)
p('The system separates access by role. A student cannot use instructor preparation features, and an instructor cannot administer platform-wide users. This separation gives content preparation, learning access, and administrative oversight clear responsibilities. Authentication, protected routes, authorization middleware, instructor approval, email verification, password reset flows, and user-management controls support this boundary.')
table(['Role','Primary Responsibilities','Key Controls'],[['Administrator','Approve instructor access; manage users; review system-level information','Admin-only routes and authorization'],['Instructor','Create sections; prepare lessons; approve material; manage quizzes; review learning evidence','Approved instructor status; section ownership'],['Student','Join sections; read released material; take quizzes; submit solution activities; review results','Verified account; section enrollment; protected student routes']])
h('2.3 Capture and Lesson Evidence',2)
p('The capture subsystem works with browser-accessible cameras and microphones while allowing prototype adapters for simulated and Raspberry Pi-connected equipment. It includes camera previews, calibration overlays, capture preflight checks, automatic-capture settings, lighting controls, audio level feedback, recording controls, and upload validation. The design exposes preparation controls because capture quality affects downstream recognition.')
p('Whiteboard images are processed through a recognition pipeline that can apply perspective-correction and quality-reliability logic before provider submission. Lesson audio is recorded, uploaded, transcoded where necessary, transcribed asynchronously, and mapped into a usable timeline. These evidence types are retained as lesson context rather than being treated as interchangeable text sources.')
h('2.4 Instructor Review and AI Assistance',2)
p('The lesson workspace presents recognized captures, transcript status, lesson context readiness, and AI-assisted preparation tools. The instructor can review uncertain expressions, select and edit context, revisit an existing lesson, and use chat assistance grounded in the active lesson. Generated lesson documents are editable through a mathematics-aware editor and can be exported after review.')
p('AI generation is constrained by context and workflow state. The platform records job status, supports retry and failure presentation, and keeps generation separate from final release. For assessments, an instructor can configure quiz generation, inspect drafts, make targeted or multi-target edits, adjust problem settings, and review generated questions before making them available to students.')
h('2.5 Student Learning and Assessment',2)
p('Students join instructor-managed sections through class codes and receive a grouped class view. They can open approved lesson material, complete assigned quizzes, view history, and upload or capture solution activities when enabled. Instructors can review solution evidence, provide feedback, inspect attempts, and use analytics to understand class-level performance. This is a direct improvement over the prior proposal, which ended at a file upload and did not provide a controlled student learning workflow.')
h('2.6 Prototype Hardware Design',2)
p('The physical prototype may use an existing classroom whiteboard, an aligned camera, optional LED lighting, a microphone, and a laptop or supported single-board setup. Hardware-specific functions are isolated behind adapters. Browser adapters support ordinary webcam and microphone operation; mock adapters support testing; and Raspberry Pi adapters support experimentation with lighting and sensor hardware. This preserves the low-cost retrofit goal while avoiding dependency on a single device model.')
table(['Component','Purpose','Design Consideration'],[['Camera','Capture whiteboard evidence','Fixed and calibrated framing reduces perspective error'],['Lighting and sensor','Improve capture consistency','Manual or automatic support; avoid glare and overexposure'],['Microphone','Record lesson audio for transcription','Level feedback and failure reporting before recording'],['Processing host','Run browser client and backend services','Prototype can use standard classroom computing hardware'],['Network connection','Reach AI and email services when configured','The interface must clearly handle unavailable services']])
h('2.7 Evaluation Plan',2)
p('The study will use developmental research guided by iterative prototyping. Requirements will be refined through consultations with mathematics instructors and pilot users. The research team will test the integrated workflow using representative classroom scenarios, then evaluate the prototype with selected instructors and students. No claim of full institutional deployment will be made before appropriate approvals, privacy review, and usability evaluation are completed.')
table(['Evaluation Area','Evidence','Indicative Measure'],[['Functional suitability','Completed role and workflow scenarios','Scenario completion and defect log'],['Recognition and context quality','Reviewed captures and transcript samples','Correction rate, completeness review, unresolved uncertainty'],['Performance efficiency','Timed capture, processing, and publication workflows','Median and range of completion times'],['Usability','Instructor and student task sessions','Task success, Likert ratings, qualitative feedback'],['Reliability','Retry, failure, recovery, and storage scenarios','Successful recovery and clear error feedback'],['Learning workflow','Quiz attempts, solution activities, and feedback review','Access correctness and instructor assessment of usefulness'],['Security and privacy','Access-control and retention review','Unauthorized-access checks and documented safeguards']])
h('2.8 Relevant Standards',2)
p('The revised standard set expands the original proposal quality focus to reflect the implemented role-based and cloud-connected system. The standards are used as evaluation and design references, not as assertions of certification.')
table(['Standard or Requirement','Application to SEA IT SOLVED'],[['ISO/IEC 25010:2023','Functional suitability, performance efficiency, interaction capability, reliability, security, and maintainability of the web platform.'],['ISO/IEC 42001:2023','Human oversight, AI accountability, risk assessment, transparent review, and continual improvement of AI-assisted functions.'],['ISO/IEC 27001:2022','Risk-based protection of lesson evidence, authentication, access controls, secure configuration, and incident planning.'],['ISO/IEC 25012:2008','Accuracy, completeness, consistency, traceability, and accessibility of lesson and assessment data.'],['Republic Act No. 10173','Lawful, transparent, proportionate processing of classroom-related personal information; appropriate retention and safeguards.']])
h('3 Prototype Design')
h('3.1 Core Data Flow',2)
p('The system core data flow begins with an authenticated user and ends with a role-appropriate learning or administrative outcome. Access is checked before a workflow begins, processing is linked to a lesson or section record, and publication is an explicit instructor action. This replaces the former single-direction Google Drive flow.')
table(['Input','Processing','Reviewed Output','Recipient'],[['Whiteboard capture','Image validation, correction, recognition','Reviewed lesson context','Instructor'],['Lesson audio','Secure upload, transcode, transcription','Transcript timeline and context','Instructor'],['Instructor instructions','Context-bound AI generation and editing','Lesson document and export','Instructor then enrolled students'],['Instructor quiz settings','Draft generation, question editing, release','Published quiz','Enrolled students'],['Student responses','Attempt state, solution evidence, feedback workflow','History, reviews, analytics','Student and instructor']])
h('3.2 Security by Design',2)
p('The prototype applies controls at the points where classroom data is introduced, processed, and released. Authentication and authorization restrict actions to the intended role. Instructor approval and email verification reduce inappropriate access. Upload middleware checks incoming capture, audio, lesson-material, and solution-submission files. Protected media routes and byte-range support make authorized playback possible without exposing files as unrestricted public resources. Rate limiting, AI-provider request gating, environmental configuration checks, and controlled background jobs reduce accidental overload and improve failure containment.')
p('Security controls do not remove the need for operational procedures. Deployment should use secure secrets management, approved data processors, a documented retention schedule, backup and incident procedures, and institutional privacy review before collecting real classroom materials. The deployment team must verify these procedures in the actual environment.')
h('3.3 Accessibility and Usability',2)
p('The interface is intended to be usable by instructors and students with varied familiarity with AI tools. Design priorities include clear workflow states, explanatory status labels, visible error messages, keyboard-accessible controls, readable mathematics rendering, responsive layouts, and reduced-motion support on public surfaces. The system should explain what a processing status means and what action a user can take instead of presenting opaque technical errors.')
h('4 Intellectual Property and Legal Considerations')
h('4.1 Ownership and Licensing',2)
p('The project documentation, application source code, interface design, and original documentation created by the research team should be identified with a clear ownership and contribution record. Third-party libraries, model-service SDKs, icons, fonts, and datasets must retain their applicable licenses and notices. The team should maintain a software bill of materials or equivalent dependency inventory before formal deployment. Any future code release should specify an explicit license rather than assuming that source visibility grants reuse rights.')
p('Lesson captures, instructor-authored notes, student submissions, and feedback may have separate ownership or institutional-use rules. The system should not imply that an AI provider owns classroom content merely because it processes a request. Institutional agreements and provider terms should be reviewed before production use, particularly where external AI services receive image, audio, or text content.')
h('4.2 Data Privacy',2)
p('Republic Act No. 10173, the Philippine Data Privacy Act of 2012, applies to processing personal information and requires transparency, legitimate purpose, and proportionality. For this project, the minimum necessary classroom data should be collected; users should be informed of the purpose and data flow; access should be limited by role; and retention should be set to a documented period. Student and instructor identifiers, recordings, and submissions should not be copied into a research report or exported outside the approved process without necessary authorization.')
p('Before classroom evaluation, the research team should prepare a privacy notice and consent or authority process appropriate to institutional research and data-governance requirements. If the system uses cloud transcription or generative AI services, the notice should identify those processors, categories of data sent, purpose, retention implications, safeguards, and the channels through which data subjects can exercise applicable rights.')
h('4.3 Responsible AI',2)
p('The system uses generative and recognition services as supporting tools, not independent instructional authorities. The instructor reviews lesson context, can edit output, chooses whether to create or release material, and remains accountable for final academic content. The interface should disclose when content is AI-assisted, surface uncertainty or provider failures, avoid presenting generated solutions as verified proof, and preserve the capture or context from which a result was generated.')
p('The research evaluation should actively look for mathematical inaccuracies, biased or inappropriate language, omissions, privacy leakage, and unsafe reliance by users. Identified issues should be logged, mitigated, and retested. This aligns with ISO/IEC 42001 and National Privacy Commission guidance on AI systems processing personal data.')
h('4.4 Research Ethics and Consultation',2)
p('Participation in user evaluation must be voluntary and based on institutionally approved procedures. Consultation and survey forms should state the study purpose, expected tasks, data collected, foreseeable risks, confidentiality measures, right to withdraw where applicable, and contact information for the research team and adviser. The appendices provide draft instruments that must be reviewed and approved before use.')
